{
  "subtype": "what_if",
  "outcome_variable": "incident_resolution_efficiency",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "resolution_time_hours",
      "causal_role": "outcome",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "Resolution time is a direct operationalization of resolution efficiency itself (the focus), not a treatment or confounder. It is essentially the outcome measure restated."
    },
    {
      "name": "priority_level",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Priority is set by triage and influences both how incidents are handled (e.g. reassignment/assignment decisions) and resolution efficiency. It is a plausible common cause not named in the query. It could also be argued to be a partially intervenable lever, but functions primarily as a confounder here."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_reassigned",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Reassignment is an actionable, settable operational lever: management can decide whether/how often to reassign tickets, making it a genuine intervenable treatment on resolution efficiency."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_efficiency_tier",
      "causal_role": "outcome",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "A bucketed (Slow/Medium/Fast) version of resolution efficiency; it is a restatement/binning of the outcome, not a treatment or confounder."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["is_reassigned"],
  "treatment_intervenable": true,
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "priority_level", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.7}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.4,
  "rationale": "The set contains a genuine intervenable treatment (is_reassigned), satisfying claim (a): reassignment is an operational decision an actor can set. However, the confounding structure (claim b) is weak. Two of the four added columns (resolution_time_hours, resolution_efficiency_tier) merely restate/bin the outcome and contribute nothing to confounder adjustment. Only priority_level serves as a plausible common cause of both reassignment and resolution efficiency, and it is a single, fairly obvious confounder. There is no adjustment for other common causes such as assignment_group workload/skill, category complexity, or caller/agent factors — precisely the kind of not-named-in-query confounders that would demonstrate the capability. Thus the counterfactual effect of reassignment cannot be cleanly read off; the interventional setup is only partially realized."
}

